<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Empires of the Sea</h1><p class="ws-lede">States whose power rested on fleets, ports and sea lanes, from Carthage to the British Empire.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/empires-of-the-sea">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Athens</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Srivijaya Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Salamis</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Punic Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Portuguese Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Republic of Venice</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Latin</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Republic</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Thalassocracy</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;The term for a state built on the sea rather than on land, which needed less bureaucracy than a territorial empire; Srivijaya is the classic example.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Chola Empire</b>: &#8220;A Tamil dynasty of southern India that built a powerful navy, and whose raids on Srivijaya's ports in the 11th century weakened its hold on maritime trade.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>British Empire</b>: &#8220;The Royal Navy's victory at Trafalgar in 1805 secured a century of naval supremacy for the largest empire in history, which dissolved within decades after 1947.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;States whose power rested on fleets, ports and sea lanes, from Carthage to the British Empire.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/empires-of-the-sea. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Empires of the Sea</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Athens <span>from 900 BCE</span></li><li>Punic Empire <span>from 814 BCE</span></li><li>Battle of Salamis <span>480 BCE</span></li><li>Srivijaya Empire <span>from 650 CE</span></li><li>Republic of Venice <span>from 697 CE</span></li><li>Portuguese Empire <span>from 1415 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Latin</dt><dd>Latin is the language of ancient Rome, which spread across the western Mediterranean with Roman rule and became the root of the Romance languages.</dd><dt>Republic</dt><dd>A republic is a state without a monarch, governed as the public’s affair by officials elected for limited terms under law.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
